%I A264840 #27 Aug 20 2026 07:44:23
%S A264840 2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,1,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,1,1,1,
%T A264840 2,2,1,1,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,
%U A264840 2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,1,1,1,2,1,1,2,2,1,1
%N A264840 Consider the sequence {3k, k >= 1}, and write down the numbers of consecutive terms that are squarefree.
%C A264840 This is a (1,2)-sequence (every term is either 1 or 2), since out of every three consecutive multiples of 3 at least one is not squarefree (it is divisible by 9).
%C A264840 From _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 20 2026: (Start)
%C A264840 Let c_1 = 27/(4*Pi^2) = 0.68391798958577995724... (the asymptotic density of numbers k such that 3*k+1 is squarefree).
%C A264840 Let c_2 = (9/7)*Product_{p prime} (1-2/p^2) = 0.41481527006474314788... (the asymptotic density of numbers k such that 3*k+1 and 3*k+2 are both squarefree).
%C A264840 Then, the asymptotic density of the occurrences of 2 in this sequence is c_2/(2*c_1 - c_2) = 0.43526364757961381554... .
%C A264840 The asymptotic density of the occurrences of 1 in this sequence is 2*(c_1 - c_2)/(2*c_1 - c_2) = 0.56473635242038618445... .
%C A264840 The asymptotic mean of this sequence is 2*c_1/(2*c_1 - c_2) = 1.43526364757961381554... . (End)
%H A264840 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A264840/b264840.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a> (terms 1..1000 from Peter J. C. Moses)
%e A264840 The multiples of 3 are 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, 30, 33, 36, .... The squarefree ones are (3,6), (15), (21), (30,33), .... So a(1)=2, a(2)=1, a(3)=1, a(4)=2, ....
%t A264840 Map[Count[#,True]&,DeleteCases[Split[Map[SquareFreeQ[3#]&,Range[400]]],{___,False,___}]] (* _Peter J. C. Moses_, Nov 26 2015 *)
%Y A264840 Cf. A005117, A261034, A264843.
%K A264840 nonn
%O A264840 1,1
